🔄 Master Recursion: A Complete Guide
Recursion is a powerful programming technique where a function calls itself to solve a smaller instance of the same problem.

🔑 The Two Essential Pillars of Recursion
Every recursive function must have these two components, or it will run forever (causing a dreaded StackOverflowError):

Base Case (The Stop Condition):

The condition where the recursion stops.

It returns a direct answer without making any further recursive calls.

Recursive Step (The Breakdown):

The part where the function calls itself with a modified (usually smaller or simpler) input.

It moves the problem closer to the base case.

🗺️ How Recursion Works Under the Hood
When a function is called, it gets pushed onto the Call Stack (LIFO - Last In, First Out).

Forward Phase (Going Down): Each recursive call pauses and waits for the result of the next call, stacking frames on top of each other.

Backward Phase (Unwinding): Once the base case is hit, the function returns a value, and the stack unwinds as each waiting function receives its result and finishes execution.

